      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for putting together this CL series, Diana!\n\nI have a couple of questions (just to help me understand the expected behavior on the EC side):\n- Are the mux/retimer configurations on initial connect (i.e to USB SS mode) going to be controlled by the EC? I think that\u0027s what we\u0027d arrived at, but wanted to confirm.\n- Currently on nipperkin, will the EC continue to control DP alt-mode entry decisions (and subsequently the \"control\" of the mux/retimer, even if the AP actually sends the TYPEC_CONTROL_COMMAND_USB_MUX_SET HC)? If so, what host event will the EC use to inform the AP that mux/retimer state needs to be changed.",
      "revId": "48f6e378bec40a4550490367cc6ed9eb1aea9cc5",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d73f0efc_81213d83",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-06-03T19:54:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Are the mux/retimer configurations on initial connect (i.e to USB SS mode) going to be controlled by the EC? I think that\u0027s what we\u0027d arrived at, but wanted to confirm.\n\nYes, currently the EC is still driving those.  And also setting the muxes back to \"none\" on partner disconnect\n\n\u003e Currently on nipperkin, will the EC continue to control DP alt-mode entry decisions (and subsequently the \"control\" of the mux/retimer, even if the AP actually sends the TYPEC_CONTROL_COMMAND_USB_MUX_SET HC)? If so, what host event will the EC use to inform the AP that mux/retimer state needs to be changed.\n\nThe EC will keep controlling the DP mode entry process until we\u0027re ready to turn that off.  We can talk more about how we want to orchestrate it, but once the VDM host command is in place then we could just turn off the EC\"s DP module altogether, letting the AP drive the muxes and VDMs.  \n\nQuestions for you:\n- Does the AP want to set the polarity, or just have the EC auto-fill it?  There\u0027s also an option to set USB 2.0 switches in our mux set API, but it only practically applies to some very old platforms\n- Would you want a feature flag to indicate the mux control sub-command is present, or would you prefer to just test for its presence by attempting to use the command?",
      "parentUuid": "dcec28d8_80ca9e23",
      "revId": "48f6e378bec40a4550490367cc6ed9eb1aea9cc5",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e24c4f73_172761df",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1181774
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-06-03T20:55:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e The EC will keep controlling the DP mode entry process until we\u0027re ready to turn that off. \n\nOK. Will the EC send out (VIRTUAL_MUX) host events to the AP when DP alt mode is entered?\n\n\u003e Does the AP want to set the polarity, or just have the EC auto-fill it?\n\nI haven\u0027t thought about this TBH. How would it work for Intel-style situations, where the retimer is programmed by the EC, but the mux by the AP (there, orientation has been an issue in the past)?\nIt\u0027s probably fine to auto-fill for external mux+retimer situations, but the mixed use case is the one to look out for.\n\n\u003e  There\u0027s also an option to set USB 2.0 switches in our mux set API,but it only practically applies to some very old platforms\n\nI don\u0027t think we\u0027ll be porting this feature that far back in our device Chronology, so probably OK to omit?\n\n\u003e Would you want a feature flag to indicate the mux control sub-command is present,\n\nFeature flag. This makes the code paths a lot cleaner in the driver (and makes it easy for userspace to determine whether a board supports a certain feature or not).",
      "parentUuid": "d73f0efc_81213d83",
      "revId": "48f6e378bec40a4550490367cc6ed9eb1aea9cc5",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
